o chunks on a non-empty tree, or a result count that differs from the summary produces no findings, so nothing is autoclosed on a partial run. `exclude-paths` (regexes; `.git` is always excluded) and `allow` (one fingerprint per line) are human-owned; a bad `allow` entry fails the run. Findings use `route: needs-human`, severity high, and carry a runbook that says to rotate the credential first. `commit` is the scanned SHA, recorded in `.ard/<n>/issue.md`; only HEAD is scanned, and a history scanner is a follow-up (#224). The forbidden flags (`--since-commit`, `--max-depth`, `--detector-timeout`, `--archive-timeout`, `--filter-unverified`, `--filter-entropy`, `--results`) are never passed, because a partial or load-dependent result would autoclose live findings. Caveats: (1) a secret gone from HEAD is not revoked; autoclose closes the issue as completed, so the issue body tells the human to rotate first. (2) The fingerprint hash is unsalted, an offline oracle for a low-entropy value that is already in the repo at HEAD; accepted. (3) Issues on a public repo show `file:line`. (4) At `max_open` the scanner is skipped, so new leaks go unseen until a human resolves one; scanner failure streaks are posted on the status issue (#221). (5) Any future change that commits scanner logs must exclude this scanner. (6) On a public repo with GitHub secret scanning off, this scanner is the only detection channel, and it cannot stop a push. TruffleHog is AGPL-3.0: it is installed by the role's Dockerfile (or a host install) and is not in the published `ard` image or the root `Dockerfile`. Host and Docker integration tests (`go test -tags integration -run Trufflehog ./internal/roles/`, with `ARD_TRUFFLEHOG_BIN` at the Dockerfile's pinned version) prove the role against a real binary and image.\n+**The trufflehog scanner** (`scanners/trufflehog`, a `sh` script plus one `jq` program) runs `trufflehog filesystem . --json --no-update --no-color --no-verification --no-ignore-tag --fail-on-scan-errors --concurrency=2` on the current commit. It never verifies a credential against a provider. Only the detector name, file, line and a hash of the secret leave the scan: `Raw`, `RawV2`, `Redacted`, `SecretParts`, `ExtraData` and `StructuredData` stay in pipes inside `scan`. The fingerprint is `trufflehog:` plus the first 16 hex digits of SHA-256 over `ard-trufflehog-v1`, a NUL byte and the base64 of the secret (an unsalted hash, so one value gives one issue wherever it appears). The scan fails closed: a non-zero trufflehog exit, a missing `finished scanning` summary, zero chunks on a non-empty tree, or a result count that differs from the summary produces no findings, so nothing is autoclosed on a partial run. `exclude-paths` (regexes; `.git` is always excluded) and `allow` (one fingerprint per line) are human-owned; a bad `allow` entry fails the run. Findings use `route: needs-human`, severity high, and carry a runbook that says to rotate the credential first. `commit` is the scanned SHA, recorded in `.ard/<n>/issue.md`; history is scanned by `scanners/trufflehog-history`. The forbidden flags (`--since-commit`, `--max-depth`, `--detector-timeout`, `--archive-timeout`, `--filter-unverified`, `--filter-entropy`, `--results`) are never passed, because a partial or load-dependent result would autoclose live findings. Caveats: (1) a secret gone from HEAD is not revoked; autoclose closes the issue as completed, so the issue body tells the human to rotate first. (2) The fingerprint hash is unsalted, an offline oracle for a low-entropy value that is already in the repo at HEAD; accepted. (3) Issues on a public repo show `file:line`. (4) At `max_open` the scanner is skipped, so new leaks go unseen until a human resolves one; scanner failure streaks are posted on the status issue (#221). (5) Any future change that commits scanner logs must exclude this scanner. (6) On a public repo with GitHub secret scanning off, this scanner is the only detection channel, and it cannot stop a push. TruffleHog is AGPL-3.0: it is installed by the role's Dockerfile (or a host install) and is not in the published `ard` image or the root `Dockerfile`. Host and Docker integration tests (`go test -tags integration -run Trufflehog ./internal/roles/`, with `ARD_TRUFFLEHOG_BIN` at the Dockerfile's pinned version) prove the role against a real binary and image.\n+\n+**The trufflehog-history scanner** (`scanners/trufflehog-history`) runs `trufflehog git file://$ARD_HISTORY --bare` with the same flags plus no `--since-commit`, `--max-depth` or `--branch`, over every fetched ref, with the same hash salt, so a value has the same fingerprint as at HEAD. A scanner role asks for history by holding an empty file named `history` in its directory (`Role.ReadsHistory`; there is no config key, so an override cannot grant it). The executor then sets `Job.History` (scanner stage only) and mounts the controller's bare clone read-only at `/history` in Docker (host mode uses the clone path) and sets `ARD_HISTORY`. The mount includes unmerged PR branches. Commit, author and timestamp are dropped; only the detector, file, line and hash leave the scan. It never autocloses, because history does not go away. A Kubernetes executor must provide a read-only copy at the same place.\n \n **The docs scanner** (`scanners/docs`, a `sh` script plus one stdlib-only Go program, `docscan.go`, built at scan time with the job's own Go) lists undocumented exported Go identifiers. It reports, per package directory: a missing package comment; an exported function, a method on an exported receiver type (pointer, value or generic) and an exported type with no doc comment (the spec's doc, its trailing comment or the enclosing declaration's doc all count); and an exported const or var with no comment, one finding per parenthesised block that has no block comment. Struct fields, interface methods, methods on unexported types and comment wording are not checked. Every exported identifier counts, `internal/` included, and files for every platform count (build constraints are not evaluated). It skips `testdata/`, `vendor/`, `node_modules/` and dot or underscore directories, files whose names start with `_` or `.`, symlinks, `*_test.go`, `//go:build ignore` files, generated files and `package main`. The fingerprint is `docs:undoc:<dir>:<symbol>` or `docs:pkg:<dir>`; the file and line are left out, so moving a declaration within its package keeps its issue. Severity is `low` and the group is the package directory. It only parses files: it never runs repository code and needs no network. It fails closed: a parse error, unreadable file, invalid path or more than 10,000 findings fails the run with nothing filed. A human-owned `exclude` file in the role directory skips package directories (`dir/` is a directory and everything below it, anything else a glob matched against the package directory); an entry makes its findings vanish, so autoclose closes their issues. Go only for now.\n \n@@ -433,6 +436,7 @@ overrides:                   # only what differs from convention\n   scanners/error-codes:     { every: 24h, group_size: 20, autoclose: true }\n   scanners/missing-tests:   { every: 24h, max_open: 5, group_size: 10, autoclose: true }\n   scanners/trufflehog:      { every: 24h, max_open: 5, group_size: 1, autoclose: true, route: needs-human, timeout: 30m }\n+  scanners/trufflehog-history: { every: 7d, max_open: 5, group_size: 1, autoclose: false, route: needs-human, timeout: 60m }\n   scanners/lint:            { every: 24h, max_open: 3, group_size: 20, autoclose: true }\n   triage/lead:              { sidecar: vpe-specs, timeout: 15m, model: claude-opus-5-5 }\n   triage/architect:         { sidecar: vpe-specs }\n@@ -447,7 +451,7 @@ overrides:                   # only what differs from convention\n \n Scanners also take `route` (`pending`, `none` or `needs-human`). Anything unset takes its default: intake in `label` mode with `ard:pending`, scanners every 4 h with `max_open` 5 and `group_size` 10, triage every 15 min, a 30-minute job timeout, `make test`, 2 test-fix attempts, 3 review rounds and worker diff thresholds of 1,000 lines (warn) and 2,000 lines (hold). Durations accept `m`, `h` and `d`. A sidecar `repo` must be `owner/name` and its `ref` a valid git ref name (no `:`, leading `-`, `..` or spaces; an abbreviated SHA is rejected, use all 40 characters); `ard config check` reports the file and key. A worker's `timeout` and `max_budget_usd` cover the whole job (every step), and `tests: \"\"` disables its test step. Per-role keys are `every`, `max_open`, `group_size`, `autoclose`, `timeout`, `agent`, `model`, `max_budget_usd`, `budget_usd_per_day` (that role's own daily cap in USD; unset or 0 means only the repo cap applies), `sidecar`, `after`, `plan_section`, `tests`, `max_test_fixes`, `max_review_rounds`, `diff_warn_lines` and `diff_max_lines` (worker roles; positive integers, warn no more than max; above warn the PR proceeds with a note, above max it is held), `reviewers` and `when` (`paths`, or `triage` roles that ran; at least one is required). `ard config check` warns when every reviewer has a `when:` rule, because a PR matching none gets no review and stays a draft. `plan_section` is for triage roles: the `## <heading>` of `plan.md` the role owns (one line, no leading `#`, unique across triage roles). The starter roles default to Requirements (product-manager), Design (architect), UX (designer), Docs (documentation-planner) and Implementation (engineer), and a value in `config.yaml` overrides that; a triage role with none may write only its own role file. There are no per-role config files: `ard init` writes a role's starting overrides into `config.yaml` for the roles it ships. Once a second repo adopts ard, role directories can move to a shared roles repo pinned by ref, like a sidecar; a repo's own directory of the same name wins. `after` names triage roles, with no self-reference and no cycles.\n \n-**`ard init` proposes the starter `.ard/` as a PR (decided, #27).** It builds `config.yaml` and the starter roles in memory (today `scanners/cve`, `scanners/docs`, `scanners/error-codes`, `scanners/lint`, `scanners/missing-tests`, `scanners/trufflehog`, `triage/lead`, `triage/engineer`, `workers/engineer`, `reviewers/eng-review`, `reviewers/license-review`, `reviewers/product-manager-review` and `reviewers/security-review`; `intake: {mode: label}`, the protected-path defaults above, and the cve, docs, error-codes, lint, lead, engineer, worker, eng-review, security-review, license-review and product-manager-review overrides), compares them with `main`, commits them on a new branch `ard-init` cut from `main` through the effect layer and a narrow committer lane, pushes it and opens a PR. It writes nothing to the local working tree or to `main`, and `scan` is committed `100755`. Files that already exist on `main` with different contents are a conflict naming the paths; `--force` proposes overwriting them, and init never deletes anything. It refuses while `ard-init` exists on the remote, whatever its PR's state, so it never force-pushes over or stacks commits on a human's edits: merge or close the PR, delete the branch and rerun. If `main` already has every starter file it prints `nothing to propose`. It syncs the label catalogue first (like `ard labels sync`), so a failure leaves nothing pushed. With `--dry-run` the commit, push and PR are logged to the ledger instead of acting. A missing repo, credentials or `main` branch exits 1 with nothing written, and `ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN` auth is refused outside `--dry-run`. The PR body carries the one step the App cannot do: copy `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` and set the repository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` (optionally `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`). When the lint scanner is among the files, it also says the scanner fails until `.ard/scanners/lint/baseline` is committed. It also says to merge with **Squash and merge** or **Create a merge commit**, never a fast-forward from the command line, which would put the bot's commit on main's first-parent history. **Rebase and merge** is also safe: the scratch-repo run (#85) showed GitHub records the merging human as committer of a rebase-merged commit (the bot stays the author), and the bot-commit check keys on committer, so it does not flag it. The body still names only the two merges above.\n+**`ard init` proposes the starter `.ard/` as a PR (decided, #27).** It builds `config.yaml` and the starter roles in memory (today `scanners/cve`, `scanners/docs`, `scanners/error-codes`, `scanners/lint`, `scanners/missing-tests`, `scanners/trufflehog`, `scanners/trufflehog-history`, `triage/lead`, `triage/engineer`, `workers/engineer`, `reviewers/eng-review`, `reviewers/license-review`, `reviewers/product-manager-review` and `reviewers/security-review`; `intake: {mode: label}`, the protected-path defaults above, and the cve, docs, error-codes, lint, lead, engineer, worker, eng-review, security-review, license-review and product-manager-review overrides), compares them with `main`, commits them on a new branch `ard-init` cut from `main` through the effect layer and a narrow committer lane, pushes it and opens a PR. It writes nothing to the local working tree or to `main`, and `scan` is committed `100755`. Files that already exist on `main` with different contents are a conflict naming the paths; `--force` proposes overwriting them, and init never deletes anything. It refuses while `ard-init` exists on the remote, whatever its PR's state, so it never force-pushes over or stacks commits on a human's edits: merge or close the PR, delete the branch and rerun. If `main` already has every starter file it prints `nothing to propose`. It syncs the label catalogue first (like `ard labels sync`), so a failure leaves nothing pushed. With `--dry-run` the commit, push and PR are logged to the ledger instead of acting. A missing repo, credentials or `main` branch exits 1 with nothing written, and `ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN` auth is refused outside `--dry-run`. The PR body carries the one step the App cannot do: copy `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` and set the repository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` (optionally `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`). When the lint scanner is among the files, it also says the scanner fails until `.ard/scanners/lint/baseline` is committed. It also says to merge with **Squash and merge** or **Create a merge commit**, never a fast-forward from the command line, which would put the bot's commit on main's first-parent history. **Rebase and merge** is also safe: the scratch-repo run (#85) showed GitHub records the merging human as committer of a rebase-merged commit (the bot stays the author), and the bot-commit check keys on committer, so it does not flag it. The body still names only the two merges above.\n \n **Where `ard serve` reads config (decided, #87).** After each fetch into its own clone (`~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git`, see Local-executor details) `ard serve` resolves `origin/main` to a SHA and reads `config.yaml` and the role directories from that commit, not from the working tree. It exports them into a snapshot at `~/.ard/config/<owner>/<repo>/<sha>/`, kept 24 hours after a newer SHA replaces it, because roles run from a host path: a role directory, its `Dockerfile` and the scan worktree must all describe the one commit the tick's jobs use. Editing the host's working tree therefore changes nothing on a running controller, with one exception: a local `paused: true` is honoured (see the kill switch), and only that key is read locally. A merged change applies on the next tick without a pull. Symlinks and submodules under the role directories are rejected, and a config that cannot be fetched or does not validate counts as paused. If `origin/main` cannot be loaded at startup, `ard serve` starts paused instead of exiting, posts the load error to the status issue once per distinct error, and retries every tick; no loop runs until a config has loaded. `ard config check` validates the working tree, for authoring; `ard config check --ref <ref>` validates a commit of the local checkout (for example `--ref origin/main` after `git fetch`) without fetching.\n \ndiff --git a/internal/config/load_test.go b/internal/config/load_test.go\nindex 52f9922..0c3caba 100644\n--- a/internal/config/load_test.go\n+++ b/internal/config/load_test.go\n@@ -943,3 +943,23 @@ func TestLLMScannerRules(t *testing.T) {\n \t\t})\n \t}\n }\n+\n+func TestReadsHistory(t *testing.T) {\n+\tfsys := baseFS()\n+\tfsys[\".ard/scanners/cve/history\"] = &fstest.MapFile{Data: nil, Mode: 0o644}\n+\tfsys[\".ard/scanners/lint/history\"] = &fstest.MapFile{Data: nil, Mode: 0o644} // LLM scanner: ignored\n+\tfsys[\".ard/triage/lead/history\"] = &fstest.MapFile{Data: nil, Mode: 0o644}   // not a scanner: ignored\n+\tres := mustLoad(t, fsys)\n+\tgot := map[string]bool{}\n+\tfor _, r := range res.Roles {\n+\t\tgot[string(r.Kind)+\"/\"+r.Name] = r.ReadsHistory()\n+\t}\n+\tfor k, v := range got {\n+\t\tif want := k == \"scanner/cve\"; v != want {\n+\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"%s ReadsHistory = %v, want %v\", k, v, want)\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\tif !got[\"scanner/cve\"] {\n+\t\tt.Errorf(\"scanner/cve with a history file must read history: %v\", got)\n+\t}\n+}\ndiff --git a/internal/config/types.go b/internal/config/types.go\nindex 486cfbc..4e60bbf 100644\n--- a/internal/config/types.go\n+++ b/internal/config/types.go\n@@ -163,6 +163,22 @@ type Role struct {\n // does not. A scanner without ScanPath counts as LLM, so the check fails closed.\n func (r Role) ReadsUntrusted() bool { return r.Kind != KindScanner || r.LLMScanner() }\n \n+// ReadsHistory reports whether the role's job gets a read-only mount of the\n+// repository's git history: a deterministic scanner whose directory holds a\n+// file named history. Only the role directory (a reviewed path) can grant it;\n+// there is no config key, so an override cannot.\n+func (r Role) ReadsHistory() bool {\n+\tif r.Kind != KindScanner || r.ScanPath == \"\" {\n+\t\treturn false\n+\t}\n+\tfor _, e := range r.Extra {\n+\t\tif e == \"history\" {\n+\t\t\treturn true\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\treturn false\n+}\n+\n // LLMScanner reports whether the role is a scanner run by an agent from\n // prompt.txt rather than by a scan executable.\n func (r Role) LLMScanner() bool { return r.Kind == KindScanner && r.ScanPath == \"\" }\ndiff --git a/internal/executor/executor.go b/internal/executor/executor.go\nindex de64a97..95ca56a 100644\n--- a/internal/executor/executor.go\n+++ b/internal/executor/executor.go\n@@ -51,6 +51,10 @@ type Job struct {\n \t// scanner); only valid with Stage \"scanner\". The zero value means it\n \t// does, so a builder that forgets the field gets the host-mode warning.\n \tTrustedInput bool\n+\t// History: the job may read the repository's full git history, read-only\n+\t// (Docker only; host mode does not enforce it),\n+\t// at Paths.History (and $ARD_HISTORY). Only valid with Stage \"scanner\".\n+\tHistory bool\n }\n \n // Sidecar is a read-only companion repo the executor clones for the job.\n@@ -69,7 +73,13 @@ type File struct {\n // Paths are the locations as the job process sees them. ConfigDir is the\n // job's empty agent-CLI config dir (host: <home>/.claude; Docker:\n // /ard-config); it is empty on a handle stored before it existed.\n-type Paths struct{ Work, Role, Sidecar, ConfigDir string }\n+//\n+// History is the bare clone with all fetched refs, read-only (enforced only by\n+// the Docker mount; host mode gives the writable live clone); empty when the\n+// job has no History. A Kubernetes executor must supply a read-only copy\n+// (for example an init container running git clone --mirror, or a bundle\n+// volume) at the same place.\n+type Paths struct{ Work, Role, Sidecar, ConfigDir, History string }\n \n // Command is what to run. Paths must come from Handle.View.\n type Command struct {\n@@ -106,6 +116,7 @@ type Handle struct {\n \tSidecar             Sidecar // copied from Job; zero on a handle stored before sidecars\n \tSidecarSHA          string  // the commit the job sees; \"\" = no sidecar\n \tTrustedInput        bool    // copied from Job by Prepare\n+\tHistoryDir          string  // host path of the git history mounted read-only, \"\" = none\n \t// Scrubbed lists the Job.Scrub paths Prepare removed from the worktree.\n \t// Close-out trusts an unchanged role file only if it is listed here.\n \tScrubbed []string\ndiff --git a/internal/executor/local/docker.go b/internal/executor/local/docker.go\nindex 6c9aa03..586254d 100644\n--- a/internal/executor/local/docker.go\n+++ b/internal/executor/local/docker.go\n@@ -69,6 +69,11 @@ func (l *Local) runArgv(h executor.Handle, cmd executor.Command, stdin bool) []s\n \tif h.SidecarDir != \"\" {\n \t\ta = append(a, \"-v\", h.SidecarDir+\":/sidecar:ro\")\n \t}\n+\tif h.HistoryDir != \"\" {\n+\t\t// Never writable. git needs safe.directory for a mount owned by another user.\n+\t\ta = append(a, \"-v\", h.HistoryDir+\":/history:ro\",\n+\t\t\t\"-e\", \"GIT_CONFIG_COUNT=1\", \"-e\", \"GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0=safe.directory\", \"-e\", \"GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_0=/history\")\n+\t}\n \tif h.View.ConfigDir != \"\" {\n \t\ta = append(a, \"-v\", filepath.Join(h.Home, configDirName)+\":/ard-config\")\n \t}\ndiff --git a/internal/executor/local/docker_integration_test.go b/internal/executor/local/docker_integration_test.go\nindex 5e5cc09..db4320f 100644\n--- a/internal/executor/local/docker_integration_test.go\n+++ b/internal/executor/local/docker_integration_test.go\n@@ -86,3 +86,29 @@ func mustRead(t *testing.T, p string) []byte {\n \t}\n \treturn b\n }\n+\n+func TestDockerHistory(t *testing.T) {\n+\tif err := exec.Command(\"docker\", \"info\").Run(); err != nil {\n+\t\tt.Skip(\"docker unavailable\")\n+\t}\n+\tf := newFixture(t)\n+\tsh(t, f.repo, \"git\", \"commit\", \"-q\", \"--allow-empty\", \"-m\", \"second\")\n+\tbare := filepath.Join(t.TempDir(), \"bare.git\")\n+\tsh(t, f.repo, \"git\", \"clone\", \"-q\", \